WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:04.087
Welcome to the Federal Modernization
Workbook's developer workbook,

2
00:00:04.087 --> 00:00:05.688
Building Provider Notices.

3
00:00:05.688 --> 00:00:09.663
This is Chapter 1: the notice
record, and Azure Service Health.

4
00:00:09.663 --> 00:00:15.051
Five providers publish the same kinds of
news in five shapes, so the first job is

5
00:00:15.051 --> 00:00:20.025
one shape, so you can ask what needs
action, by when, and who owns it, just once.

6
00:00:20.025 --> 00:00:25.023
We'll start from Microsoft Learn's own
Resource Graph queries and alert policy,

7
00:00:25.023 --> 00:00:29.791
see what they leave to you, then read
the kit's record and its Azure collector,

8
00:00:29.791 --> 00:00:32.713
and walk two labs, the
evidence and the dangers.

9
00:00:32.713 --> 00:00:34.287
Two notes before we begin.

10
00:00:34.287 --> 00:00:37.650
This chapter is a draft,
awaiting the owner's review.

11
00:00:37.650 --> 00:00:41.530
And the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

12
00:00:45.280 --> 00:00:46.617
Why start with a record?

13
00:00:46.617 --> 00:00:51.189
Because a reader should be able to
ask, once, what needs action, by when,

14
00:00:51.189 --> 00:00:55.679
and who owns it, and five providers
answer in five different shapes.

15
00:00:55.679 --> 00:00:58.080
So the goal of this chapter is concrete.

16
00:00:58.080 --> 00:01:03.006
By the end you can read every Service Health
event for the subscriptions in scope,

17
00:01:03.006 --> 00:01:07.859
with the least-privileged role, into records
that say what kind of notice each is,

18
00:01:07.859 --> 00:01:12.418
whether it needs action and by when, who
said so, and which resources it names.

19
00:01:12.418 --> 00:01:15.507
And you can plan Service Health
alerts at scale as a file

20
00:01:15.507 --> 00:01:19.300
that a reviewer approves by its
hash before anything changes.

21
00:01:23.050 --> 00:01:24.413
Next, which cloud.

22
00:01:24.413 --> 00:01:29.697
The workbook's estate is a Microsoft
365 GCC tenant with commercial Azure,

23
00:01:29.697 --> 00:01:35.501
so the Azure CLI is set to Azure Cloud and
Resource Manager is the commercial host.

24
00:01:35.501 --> 00:01:38.026
Azure Government is a side note here:

25
00:01:38.026 --> 00:01:41.900
its own CLI cloud name and
its own Resource Manager host.

26
00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:46.330
Learn's sample queries link the Azure
Government portal, but a statement

27
00:01:46.330 --> 00:01:50.613
that the table is available there, in
so many words, is not yet specified.

28
00:01:50.613 --> 00:01:55.559
The kit never guesses: the settings
name the cloud, an unknown name throws,

29
00:01:55.559 --> 00:01:59.710
and the live run refuses a CLI
that's set to another cloud.

30
00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.335
Here's Microsoft's starting point.

31
00:02:05.335 --> 00:02:10.252
Microsoft Learn publishes Resource
Graph sample queries for Service Health:

32
00:02:10.252 --> 00:02:15.622
all active events, health advisories,
upcoming retirements and planned maintenance.

33
00:02:15.622 --> 00:02:18.848
A second page gives the
impacted-resources query.

34
00:02:18.848 --> 00:02:21.885
Reader on a subscription is
enough to see its events,

35
00:02:21.885 --> 00:02:26.336
but a security advisory's sensitive
fields and its impacted resources need

36
00:02:26.336 --> 00:02:30.786
Service Health Security Reader; Reader
and Monitoring Reader can't see them.

37
00:02:30.786 --> 00:02:33.045
And for alerting between collections,

38
00:02:33.045 --> 00:02:37.424
Learn names a built-in policy that
creates the Service Health alert rule in

39
00:02:37.424 --> 00:02:42.230
every subscription, with a remediation
task for the ones that already exist.

40
00:02:45.980 --> 00:02:47.343
What does Learn leave to you?

41
00:02:47.343 --> 00:02:50.567
Each sample answers one
question with its own columns,

42
00:02:50.567 --> 00:02:53.218
so there are several
queries to run and merge.

43
00:02:53.218 --> 00:02:55.855
Each event comes back
once per subscription,

44
00:02:55.855 --> 00:02:58.279
so one outage would be counted many times.

45
00:02:58.279 --> 00:03:02.592
Event types don't say what to do: a
health advisory can be a retirement.

46
00:03:02.592 --> 00:03:04.568
Nothing says action is required.

47
00:03:04.568 --> 00:03:06.695
The alert policy is portal steps,

48
00:03:06.695 --> 00:03:10.005
with nothing a reviewer
can approve before it runs.

49
00:03:10.005 --> 00:03:12.180
And nothing covers the other providers.

50
00:03:12.180 --> 00:03:14.705
Each of those gaps is pinned by a test.

51
00:03:14.705 --> 00:03:17.835
The verdict: keep Learn's
queries as the model,

52
00:03:17.835 --> 00:03:22.350
and build the record, the merge,
the kinds and the action rule.

53
00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:27.350
So here's the record.

54
00:03:27.350 --> 00:03:30.818
Every notice from every
provider becomes one record,

55
00:03:30.818 --> 00:03:33.025
defined in the kit's JSON schema.

56
00:03:33.025 --> 00:03:36.475
It has an ID made of the provider
and the provider's own ID,

57
00:03:36.475 --> 00:03:39.025
which is what the router de-duplicates on.

58
00:03:39.025 --> 00:03:43.520
It has one of six kinds, a status,
plain-text title and summary,

59
00:03:43.520 --> 00:03:49.025
the impact window in UTC, and the
services, regions and resources it names.

60
00:03:49.025 --> 00:03:53.818
The three action fields matter most:
whether action is needed, by when,

61
00:03:53.818 --> 00:03:58.275
and who said so, the provider's own
field or a stated rule of the kit.

62
00:03:58.275 --> 00:04:00.240
And it ends with a fingerprint.

63
00:04:03.990 --> 00:04:06.500
The fingerprint is the
reason the router can tell

64
00:04:06.500 --> 00:04:08.765
a changed notice from
one it has already seen.

65
00:04:08.765 --> 00:04:12.840
It's a hash of the record without the
first-seen time and without itself,

66
00:04:12.840 --> 00:04:16.212
so when a provider edits a
notice the fingerprint changes,

67
00:04:16.212 --> 00:04:20.427
and when the kit simply reads the same
notice again a week later, it doesn't.

68
00:04:20.427 --> 00:04:22.216
And a record has to be valid.

69
00:04:22.216 --> 00:04:27.095
The kit checks the schema's rules in code,
so the check runs on older PowerShell

70
00:04:27.095 --> 00:04:31.365
7 too, and the collector refuses to
write a run with an invalid record.

71
00:04:31.365 --> 00:04:33.950
Every time is stored in UTC.

72
00:04:37.700 --> 00:04:39.238
Now the Azure collector.

73
00:04:39.238 --> 00:04:44.624
It makes two declared calls, the events
query and the impacted-resources query,

74
00:04:44.624 --> 00:04:49.100
each a Resource Graph query in a
live run and a file in fixture mode.

75
00:04:49.100 --> 00:04:52.687
Then the normalizer maps
each event type to a kind.

76
00:04:52.687 --> 00:04:56.741
Service issues are incidents,
planned maintenance is maintenance,

77
00:04:56.741 --> 00:05:01.375
and a health advisory is a plain advisory
unless its subtype says retirement.

78
00:05:01.375 --> 00:05:06.873
Two kinds are action required by the kit's
rule: a retirement, due at the advisory's

79
00:05:06.873 --> 00:05:12.037
end, and a security advisory, with no
date, for the security officer to review.

80
00:05:12.037 --> 00:05:16.210
Anything else, like billing,
is left out and counted.

81
00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:24.535
For alerting between collections, the
kit follows Learn's built-in policy,

82
00:05:24.535 --> 00:05:26.860
but as a plan instead of portal clicks.

83
00:05:26.860 --> 00:05:30.248
The script reads the built-in
definition and checks its name.

84
00:05:30.248 --> 00:05:33.445
It writes a plan with two
steps: the policy assignment,

85
00:05:33.445 --> 00:05:36.861
with a managed identity that
holds the definition's role,

86
00:05:36.861 --> 00:05:40.785
and a remediation task for the
subscriptions that already exist.

87
00:05:40.785 --> 00:05:44.352
Learn's remediation page notes
that only the portal grants

88
00:05:44.352 --> 00:05:47.847
that role automatically, so
the plan names the role itself.

89
00:05:47.847 --> 00:05:51.816
The script refuses a resource group
scope, prints the plan's hash,

90
00:05:51.816 --> 00:05:55.990
and changes nothing unless it's
run again with that exact hash.

91
00:05:59.740 --> 00:06:00.840
Who is involved?

92
00:06:00.840 --> 00:06:03.353
Developers build and maintain the kit.

93
00:06:03.353 --> 00:06:05.961
The cloud platform lead owns this gate:

94
00:06:05.961 --> 00:06:09.791
the reader's roles, the alert
policy and its change record.

95
00:06:09.791 --> 00:06:14.590
And the security officer keeps the
evidence and reviews security advisories.

96
00:06:14.590 --> 00:06:16.477
What the reader may hold is short.

97
00:06:16.477 --> 00:06:20.453
Two calls, each needing Reader
on each subscription in scope.

98
00:06:20.453 --> 00:06:25.189
Service Health Security Reader is named
as the elevated role, because without

99
00:06:25.189 --> 00:06:29.927
it a security advisory is listed but its
summary and impacted resources aren't.

100
00:06:29.927 --> 00:06:32.320
And nothing the reader holds can write.

101
00:06:36.070 --> 00:06:40.514
Lab 1 needs only PowerShell 7 with
Pester, working in the kit's folder,

102
00:06:40.514 --> 00:06:44.482
against two weeks of fictional
notices from every provider.

103
00:06:44.482 --> 00:06:45.408
No tenant.

104
00:06:45.408 --> 00:06:47.267
Step 1 lists what Azure needs:

105
00:06:47.267 --> 00:06:50.832
this is what a reviewer reads
before granting anything.

106
00:06:50.832 --> 00:06:54.820
Two calls, each needing Reader
on each subscription in scope,

107
00:06:54.820 --> 00:06:57.532
each linked to the
Learn page that says so.

108
00:06:57.532 --> 00:07:00.045
Step 2 prints the kit's events query.

109
00:07:00.045 --> 00:07:04.702
Compare it with Learn's all-active sample:
the same filter, with the retirement

110
00:07:04.702 --> 00:07:07.321
sample's subtype added,
the times converted,

111
00:07:07.321 --> 00:07:10.160
and named columns projected and ordered.

112
00:07:13.910 --> 00:07:17.098
Step 3 collects Azure from
the day-one fixtures.

113
00:07:17.098 --> 00:07:20.101
Five notices, all open,
two needing action,

114
00:07:20.101 --> 00:07:24.872
one of each kind but change, and
every one valid against the schema.

115
00:07:24.872 --> 00:07:27.672
Notice: five notices from six rows.

116
00:07:27.672 --> 00:07:31.422
The incident's rows from two
subscriptions became one notice.

117
00:07:31.422 --> 00:07:33.922
Step 4 reads the records as a table.

118
00:07:33.922 --> 00:07:37.549
The retirement of basic
public IP addresses

119
00:07:37.549 --> 00:07:40.784
is action required by September 30, 2027.

120
00:07:40.784 --> 00:07:44.397
The security advisory is
action required with no date.

121
00:07:44.397 --> 00:07:48.672
The incident, the advisory and the
planned maintenance need no action,

122
00:07:48.672 --> 00:07:51.100
and they'll go to the weekly digest.

123
00:07:54.850 --> 00:07:57.337
Step 5 reads the retirement in full.

124
00:07:57.337 --> 00:08:02.103
Look at the action basis: it names the
kit's rule, an Azure retirement due at

125
00:08:02.103 --> 00:08:07.026
the advisory's end time, not Microsoft,
as the reason the notice needs action.

126
00:08:07.026 --> 00:08:11.187
So nobody argues with Microsoft
about a date Microsoft never set.

127
00:08:11.187 --> 00:08:15.249
The source link is the event on the
commercial Resource Manager host.

128
00:08:15.249 --> 00:08:20.037
Step 6 reads the same fixtures a day
later and compares the fingerprints.

129
00:08:20.037 --> 00:08:23.875
The first-seen time changes in
the run, but no fingerprint does.

130
00:08:23.875 --> 00:08:28.220
The last line says zero differ, so
the router will see nothing new.

131
00:08:31.970 --> 00:08:35.008
Step 7 plans the Service
Health alert policy.

132
00:08:35.008 --> 00:08:37.990
The production fixture
names a management group,

133
00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:42.718
so the plan assigns the built-in definition
there and remediates the existing

134
00:08:42.718 --> 00:08:47.519
subscriptions: two would-apply steps, the
plan's hash, and dry run, nothing changed.

135
00:08:47.519 --> 00:08:52.257
The role ID in the fixture is made
up; Lab 2 reads the real one.

136
00:08:52.257 --> 00:08:54.977
Step 8 tries to apply it in fixture mode,

137
00:08:54.977 --> 00:08:58.632
and the script refuses before
it even reads the hash.

138
00:08:58.632 --> 00:09:03.432
Step 9 runs this chapter's tests:
twenty-six passed, none failed.

139
00:09:03.432 --> 00:09:05.630
Then delete the output folder.

140
00:09:09.380 --> 00:09:13.187
Lab 2 moves to an Azure test
subscription in a test tenant,

141
00:09:13.187 --> 00:09:17.318
never production, and an empty
list of events is a valid result.

142
00:09:17.318 --> 00:09:20.751
Grant the reader its two roles,
sign in in the right cloud,

143
00:09:20.751 --> 00:09:25.080
and cross-check: Learn's own query
and the kit's collection should agree.

144
00:09:25.080 --> 00:09:28.353
Then plan the alert policy
at the test subscription,

145
00:09:28.353 --> 00:09:31.855
have a second person approve
the hash, apply it, and check.

146
00:09:31.855 --> 00:09:36.811
Learn says the remediation creates a
resource group, an alert rule and an action

147
00:09:36.811 --> 00:09:39.289
group that emails subscription owners;

148
00:09:39.289 --> 00:09:42.218
Service Health alerts
are activity log alerts.

149
00:09:42.218 --> 00:09:44.140
Finally, back it all out.

150
00:09:47.890 --> 00:09:52.690
Each output is evidence for something
specific, against NIST's control catalog.

151
00:09:52.690 --> 00:09:55.785
The reader's roles, and the
fact that nothing can write,

152
00:09:55.785 --> 00:09:58.740
support monitoring an
external service provider.

153
00:09:58.740 --> 00:10:01.952
The run folder shows every
event in scope was read,

154
00:10:01.952 --> 00:10:05.790
which supports receiving
security alerts and advisories.

155
00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:10.290
Valid records and unchanged fingerprints
support continuous monitoring.

156
00:10:10.290 --> 00:10:12.766
The approved alert plan supports the same,

157
00:10:12.766 --> 00:10:17.790
and an approved rollback is configuration
change control and contingency planning.

158
00:10:17.790 --> 00:10:20.261
The key security indicators are FedRAMP's,

159
00:10:20.261 --> 00:10:24.215
and which output supports which
indicator is the program's mapping.

160
00:10:24.215 --> 00:10:29.250
The kit's records support these controls;
the agency's assessor decides them.

161
00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:35.875
What breaks in production,
and what guards against it?

162
00:10:35.875 --> 00:10:39.292
Read security advisories
with Reader alone and you

163
00:10:39.292 --> 00:10:42.550
see the advisory but not
its summary or resources.

164
00:10:42.550 --> 00:10:47.375
Read from the wrong cloud and you get nothing,
and the gate passes on an empty list.

165
00:10:47.375 --> 00:10:51.463
Count an event per subscription
and one outage becomes five tasks.

166
00:10:51.463 --> 00:10:55.900
Read a retirement as an advisory
and the deadline passes unnoticed.

167
00:10:55.900 --> 00:11:00.988
Mistake a kit rule for Microsoft's and you
argue about a date Microsoft never set.

168
00:11:00.988 --> 00:11:05.025
Apply the policy unreviewed and
resources appear everywhere.

169
00:11:05.025 --> 00:11:10.400
And per Learn, remediation may
overwrite hand edits to the alert rules.

170
00:11:14.150 --> 00:11:15.500
Here's what done looks like.

171
00:11:15.500 --> 00:11:18.076
The reader holds Reader
everywhere in scope,

172
00:11:18.076 --> 00:11:22.950
the security reader role where security
advisories matter, and nothing that writes.

173
00:11:22.950 --> 00:11:24.613
The settings name the cloud.

174
00:11:24.613 --> 00:11:27.224
Learn's query and the
kit's collection agree.

175
00:11:27.224 --> 00:11:30.092
Every record validates,
and says whether the

176
00:11:30.092 --> 00:11:33.037
provider or a kit rule
made it action required.

177
00:11:33.037 --> 00:11:37.774
The alert plan's hash was approved by
someone other than the person who applies it,

178
00:11:37.774 --> 00:11:40.955
the alert rule exists in
each subscription untouched,

179
00:11:40.955 --> 00:11:43.075
and the rollback has been rehearsed.

180
00:11:43.075 --> 00:11:47.812
Next, Chapter 2 fills the same
record from Microsoft 365.

181
00:11:47.812 --> 00:11:48.970
Thanks for watching.
